"We are given the following information:\n\n- Total number of students = 20\n- 20% enrolled in **contemporary** dance.\n- 25% of the **remaining** students enrolled in **jazz** dance.\n- The **rest** enrolled in **hip-hop** dance.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the number of students in **contemporary** dance.\n\n20% of 20 students:\n$$\n0.20 \\times 20 = 4 \\text{ students}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Find the number of students **remaining** after contemporary.\n\n$$\n20 - 4 = 16 \\text{ students}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the number of students in **jazz** dance.\n\n25% of the **remaining** 16 students:\n$$\n0.25 \\times 16 = 4 \\text{ students}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Find the number of students in **hip-hop** dance.\n\n$$\n20 - 4 - 4 = 12 \\text{ students}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Find the **percentage** of students in **hip-hop** dance.\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{20} \\times 100 = 60\\%\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Answer: 60**"
"We are given the following:\n\n- The merchant has two options to purchase:\n  - **Jewelry** worth **$5,000**\n  - **Electronic gadgets** worth **$8,000**\n\n- The **jewelry market will rise by 2.5%**.\n- The **electronic gadgets market will rise by 1.2%**.\n\nThe goal is to **maximize profit** at the end of the month, so we need to calculate the **profit** from each option and choose the one with the **higher profit**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the profit from **jewelry**\n\n- Initial value: $5,000\n- Market rise: 2.5% = 0.025\n\n$$\n\\text{Profit from jewelry} = 5000 \\times 0.025 = 125\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the profit from **electronic gadgets**\n\n- Initial value: $8,000\n- Market rise: 1.2% = 0.012\n\n$$\n\\text{Profit from gadgets} = 8000 \\times 0.012 = 96\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Compare the profits\n\n- Profit from jewelry: **$125**\n- Profit from gadgets: **$96**\n\nThe **jewelry** option gives a **higher profit**.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{125}\n$$\n\n**Answer: 125**"
